# Target C — Section-146 `q-k` corollary and endpoint exclusion

**Date:** 2026-09-12  
**Lifecycle:** proof-only consequence of audited-working §§142–146; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

The audited-working structural omnibus proves in §146 the Type-II inequality

\[
\liminf {1\over D}
\left(
 h-{d_H\over9}-{10s_Z\over9}
\right)
\ge
c_*,
\qquad
c_*:={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right),
\quad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3}.
\tag{QC.1}
\]

This note records a missed exact corollary and then excludes its equality endpoint.
The first observation is algebraic:

\[
q-k=h-s_Z
=\left(h-{d_H\over9}-{10s_Z\over9}\right)
+{d_H+s_Z\over9}.
\tag{QC.2}
\]

Since `k=d_H+s_Z>=0`, §146 already implies

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {q-k\over D}
\ge c_*
={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}\right)
=1.8195135379794445\ldots .
}
\tag{QC.3}
\]

This is much stronger than the separately corrected `1.637562...` proof-only floor.
It does **not** revive the rejected `1.9046256...` claim.

The main new theorem below shows that even (QC.3) cannot be asymptotically sharp.
The mechanism is the equality structure already latent in §142: the endpoint forces a
positive atomic train-direction mass, while the outer contact walk is forced to be
asymptotically perimeter-minimizing around the near-disk force hull. Such a walk has
uniform projective edge-direction measure and therefore cannot carry any atomic mass.

No source point is deleted. Contact-isolated coordinates never enter the Section-57
affine-kernel theorem.

---

## 1. The missed Section-146 corollary

Recall

\[
d_H=q-h,
\qquad
k=d_H+s_Z.
\]

Then

\[
q-k=q-d_H-s_Z=h-s_Z.
\]

Subtract the left side of (QC.1):

\[
(h-s_Z)-\left(h-{d_H\over9}-{10s_Z\over9}\right)
={d_H+s_Z\over9}={k\over9}\ge0.
\tag{QC.4}
\]

Taking liminf proves (QC.3) immediately. This uses no new geometric input.

---

## 2. Equality in (QC.3) forces the full §146 scalar stack to saturate

Put

\[
s_*:=\sqrt{\Delta_2},
\qquad
c_*={4\over9}(\pi+s_*),
\qquad
\phi_*:={8s_*-\pi\over9}=2c_*-\pi.
\tag{QC.5}
\]

Numerically

\[
s_*=0.9523128068\ldots,
\qquad
\phi_*=0.4974344224\ldots .
\]

Assume for contradiction that an unbounded Type-II survivor sequence satisfies

\[
{q-k\over D}\to c_*.
\tag{QC.6}
\]

Let

\[
E_D:=h-{d_H\over9}-{10s_Z\over9}.
\]

By (QC.2),

\[
q-k=E_D+{k\over9}.
\tag{QC.7}
\]

Audited-working §146 gives `liminf E_D/D>=c_*`. Equation (QC.6) and `k>=0` therefore
force

\[
\boxed{
{k\over D}\to0,
\qquad
{d_H\over D}\to0,
\qquad
{s_Z\over D}\to0,
\qquad
{h\over D},{q\over D}\to c_*.
}
\tag{QC.8}
\]

Audited-working (146.2) then gives

\[
\liminf {f\over D}
\ge s_*-{c_*\over4}
={8s_*-\pi\over9}
=\phi_*.
\tag{QC.9}
\]

The exact outer-walk ledger is

\[
b=2h-Q-2s_Z.
\tag{QC.10}
\]

Canonical Type II gives `per(F)/D->pi` and `per(F)<=b`; hence

\[
\limsup {Q\over D}
\le2c_*-\pi
=\phi_*.
\tag{QC.11}
\]

Since `f<=Q`, (QC.9)--(QC.11) force simultaneous equality:

\[
\boxed{
{f\over D},{Q\over D}\to\phi_*,
\qquad
{Q-f\over D}\to0,
\qquad
{b\over D}\to\pi,
\qquad
{b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}\to0.
}
\tag{QC.12}
\]

Thus asymptotically all exceptional-face degree excess is the mandatory one unit per
face: all but `o(D)` exceptional faces are pentagons. More importantly for the present
argument, the outer contact walk is asymptotically perimeter-minimizing around the
near-disk force hull.

---

## 3. Equality also fixes the atomic/non-atomic train split

Use audited-working §142 notation. Let `T` be the number of complete stopped trains,
including singleton trains, and let `mu_D` be their empirical projective direction
measure. Pass to a subsequence on which

\[
{T\over D}\to x,
\qquad
{q\over T}\to y,
\qquad
\mu_D\Rightarrow\mu.
\tag{QC.13}
\]

Let `a in [0,1]` be the total atomic mass of `mu`.

The exact stopped-train ledger is

\[
T=q+2f-k=(q-k)+2f.
\tag{QC.14}
\]

Therefore (QC.6) and (QC.12) give

\[
\boxed{
x=c_*+2\phi_*={2\pi+20s_*\over9}.}
\tag{QC.15}
\]

Also

\[
\boxed{y={c_*\over x}.}
\tag{QC.16}
\]

Audited-working §142 proves

\[
2s_*\le xa
\tag{QC.17}
\]

and

\[
y\le2(1-a).
\tag{QC.18}
\]

But direct substitution of (QC.15)--(QC.16) gives

\[
x\left(1-{y\over2}\right)
=x-{c_*\over2}
=2s_*.
\tag{QC.19}
\]

Combining (QC.17)--(QC.19),

\[
2s_*
\le xa
\le x\left(1-{y\over2}\right)
=2s_*.
\]

Hence both inequalities saturate:

\[
\boxed{
a={2s_*\over x}=1-{y\over2}.}
\tag{QC.20}
\]

Equivalently the non-atomic train fraction is exactly

\[
1-a={y\over2},
\]

so the number of non-atomic trains has normalized limit

\[
\boxed{
{(1-a)T\over D}\to {c_*\over2}.
}
\tag{QC.21}
\]

Numerically the limiting atomic and non-atomic fractions are approximately

\[
a=0.6767472769\ldots,
\qquad
1-a=0.3232527231\ldots .
\]

The proof below uses only the exact relation (QC.21), not these decimals.

---

## 4. Near-geodesic outer walks around a disk have uniform projective edge-direction measure

For every outer contact-walk edge occurrence `e`, let

\[
\phi_e\in\mathbb R/\pi\mathbb Z
\]

be its projective unit-edge direction. Define the finite measure

\[
\nu_D:={1\over D}\sum_{e\in\partial_{out}}\delta_{\phi_e}
\tag{QC.22}
\]

on the projective direction circle. Its total mass is `b/D->pi` by (QC.12).

### Lemma QC.1 — uniform direction measure

Under Type-II near-disk convergence and (QC.12),

\[
\boxed{
\nu_D\Rightarrow d\phi
}
\tag{QC.23}
\]

where `d phi` is Lebesgue measure on `R/pi Z`.

### Proof

Write the occurrence-counted outer walk as

\[
p_0,p_1,\ldots,p_b=p_0,
\qquad |p_{j+1}-p_j|=1.
\]

All loaded force-hull vertices occur on the outer walk and every walk vertex lies in
`F`, so the convex hull of the walk vertices is exactly `F`.

For `n_theta=(cos theta,sin theta)`, define

\[
V_D(\theta)
:=\sum_j |(p_{j+1}-p_j)\cdot n_\theta|
=D\int |\cos(\theta-\phi)|\,d\nu_D(\phi).
\tag{QC.24}
\]

The projected closed walk visits the minimum and maximum support values of `F`, so

\[
V_D(\theta)\ge2w_F(\theta).
\tag{QC.25}
\]

Integrating over `theta in [0,pi]`,

\[
\int_0^\pi V_D(\theta)d\theta=2b,
\qquad
\int_0^\pi w_F(\theta)d\theta=\operatorname{per}(F)
\tag{QC.26}
\]

by the elementary cosine integral and Cauchy's perimeter formula. Therefore

\[
\int_0^\pi[V_D(\theta)-2w_F(\theta)]d\theta
=2[b-\operatorname{per}(F)]=o(D).
\tag{QC.27}
\]

The integrand is nonnegative. Canonical Section 80 gives the uniform width limit

\[
\sup_\theta|w_F(\theta)/D-1|\to0.
\tag{QC.28}
\]

Hence

\[
{V_D\over D}\to2
\quad\text{in }L^1([0,\pi]).
\tag{QC.29}
\]

Take any weakly convergent subsequence `nu_D -> nu`. The masses are uniformly bounded,
and the kernel `|cos(theta-phi)|` is continuous and 1-Lipschitz in `theta`, so the
cosine transforms are equicontinuous. Equation (QC.29) forces

\[
\int |\cos(\theta-\phi)|d\nu(\phi)=2
\quad\text{for every }\theta.
\tag{QC.30}
\]

The transform is injective on finite measures on the projective circle. A direct
proof is distributional. For

\[
g(\theta)=|\cos\theta|
\]

as a `pi`-periodic distribution,

\[
g''+g=2\delta_{\pi/2\pmod\pi}.
\tag{QC.31}
\]

Apply `d^2/dtheta^2+1` to (QC.30). The left side becomes twice a translate of `nu`,
while the same operator applied to the constant `2` is the constant density `2`.
Thus `nu` is Lebesgue measure. Every subsequential weak limit is the same, proving
(QC.23). `square`

In particular no fixed finite collection of shrinking direction arcs can carry a
positive normalized number of outer-walk occurrences.

---

## 5. Atomic trains do not have enough non-atomic partners to supply the outer walk

Choose finitely many atoms of `mu` carrying train mass at least `a-epsilon`, and take
disjoint `delta`-neighborhoods of their projective directions. Along the subsequence
in (QC.13), the number of trains outside these neighborhoods is at most

\[
(1-a+\epsilon+o(1))T.
\tag{QC.32}
\]

Every complete stopped train has at most two outer-face edge occurrences; this is the
same endpoint-count fact used in audited-working §142. Therefore outer occurrences
belonging to trains outside the selected atomic neighborhoods number at most

\[
2(1-a+\epsilon+o(1))T.
\tag{QC.33}
\]

By (QC.21), after sending the source size to infinity and then `epsilon->0`, the
normalized capacity of the entire non-atomic train population is only

\[
\boxed{c_*D+o(D)}
\tag{QC.34}
\]

outer occurrences.

But the complete outer walk has

\[
b=\pi D+o(D).
\tag{QC.35}
\]

Since

\[
\pi-c_*>0,
\tag{QC.36}
\]

at least

\[
(\pi-c_*-o(1))D
\tag{QC.37}
\]

outer occurrences must belong to trains in the selected atom neighborhoods. Now send
`delta->0` after the source-size limit. Equation (QC.37) forces a positive atomic mass
in a weak limit of the outer-edge direction measures `nu_D`.

An outer edge occurrence belonging to a selected train has the same projective
direction as that train: every contact edge in one auxiliary stopped-train component
is parallel by the construction in §142.1. Thus shrinking the selected train-phase
neighborhoods also shrinks the corresponding outer-edge direction neighborhoods; no
unproved relation between a train phase and an outer tangent is being used.

This contradicts Lemma QC.1, which says that every such limit is the nonatomic
Lebesgue measure.

Therefore (QC.6) is impossible.

---

## 6. Main theorem

### Theorem QC.2 — the audited-working `1.8195135...` `q-k` floor is not attainable

Assuming the audited-working §§142–146 module, every Type-II C-II survivor sequence
satisfies

\[
\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge c_*,
\]

and there is **no** unbounded Type-II survivor sequence for which

\[
\boxed{
{q-k\over D}\to
c_*={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\frac\pi{2\sqrt3}}\right).
}
\tag{QC.38}
\]

Thus the strengthened proof-only floor has a structural, not merely numerical,
endpoint gap.

As with any sequence-defined Type-II endpoint exclusion, a uniform positive separation
from `c_*` follows only in the corresponding diagonal/compactness formulation. No
finite explicit epsilon is claimed here.

---

## 7. Scope and remaining obstruction

- The proof depends on audited-working §142 and §146; it is not canonical theorem-state
  mathematics.
- The exact stopped-train ledger retains singleton trains, bridge occurrences, bounded
  defect faces, and positive `s_Z` before endpoint saturation forces the latter
  sublinear.
- Every source point is retained.
- The macroscopic inactive-container branch is untouched.
- Type-II sequences bounded away from the endpoint remain possible.
- No strict sub-`D` source is constructed and no exact unit triangle is forced.

**Disposition:** proof-only downstream theorem awaiting hostile audit. C-II remains
open.
